From 9ac621ef5ac795ba46b95428db539fd6ff00a0ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Guilhem Bichot <guilhem.bichot@contributor>

Date: Thu, 11 Sep 2025 16:54:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] decrease the memory footprint of session
 plan cache.

( Note: in the patches I added some more '#ifdef PLAN_CACHE_TAURUS'.
These ifdefs make code a bit hard to read. I plan to keep them for few
more months, at least until the PQ code goes into the dstore branch,
until we decide if plan cache has to use PQ code again (like it did
before, and like it does not anymore), until plan cache is used in
production and validated by customer use (telling if my approach to do
without PQ code made a working feature or not). Once there, we can
make final decisions and perhaps remove the ifdefs. )

Allocate cached plan into dedicated MEM_ROOT to decrease memory usage
when there are invalidations.

Before this patch, as the cached plan was allocated in the prepared
statement's MEM_ROOT, if the plan got invalidated we could not free
it. This could cause ever-increasing memory usage, which we prevented
by re-preparing the statement after 5 invalidations.

So we observed the following behaviour in a long sysbench run. Memory
usage grew until all 320000 prepared statements had a cached
plan (expected), then it grew again very slowly for more hours then
stabilized. The reason for the second growth phase was this:
invalidations happened, though rarely, causing a slow increase, until
all statements got 5 invalidations, then that was the maximum
consumption.

Now, with this patch, there is a dedicated MEM_ROOT which is freed at
first invalidation and the prepared statement is not re-prepared,
which is more efficient. As a consequence there also needs to be a
dedicated Query_arena.

Expose the memory usage in performance.schema, one can use:
SELECT * FROM
performance_schema.memory_summary_by_thread_by_event_name WHERE
event_name="memory/sql/plan_cache_mem_root";

In the mtr test:
- we check memory usage
- some EXECUTE are removed as we neither want nor can trigger
statement's re-preparation anymore, so the invalidation count for the
rest of the test is reduced by 7.
- a test with a JSON function is added, because it exposed a mistake
(a memory leak) in the initial version of this patch.
- test group_replication.gr_assert_psi_memory_instruments_disabled is
reading a row from the Performance_schema.setup_instruments, about key
memory/group_rpl/Gcs_message_data::m_buffer, and did not find it
anymore. This is because the said table has a max size of 450, and
this limit was already reached before this patch. As this patch adds
one key, which is registered early, the table is already full when
registering the Gcs key, which thus is not inserted into the table.
Fix: increase the default table's size from 450 to 454.
